1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,270
Welcome to practicals eleven and twelve:

2
00:00:03,270 --> 00:00:05,885
multivariate analysis in Python.

3
00:00:05,885 --> 00:00:16,512
We use K-Means for structure discovery, the silhouette score, visualising groups, and pairplots, on the Superstore orders dataset.

4
00:00:17,312 --> 00:00:30,024
New in this lab are the variance inflation factor function from stats models, Standard Scaler for scaling, K Means for clustering, and silhouette score for cluster quality.

5
00:00:30,024 --> 00:00:34,976
We load the Superstore data and drop the rows with a blank segment.

6
00:00:35,776 --> 00:00:38,376
Same as practical nine and ten:

7
00:00:38,376 --> 00:00:44,246
select the numeric columns and drop postal code, because it is an I D.

8
00:00:44,246 --> 00:00:50,368
We use sales, quantity, discount and profit together throughout this lab.

9
00:00:51,168 --> 00:00:56,805
Step one for multicollinearity is the correlation matrix and heatmap.

10
00:00:56,805 --> 00:01:03,014
Look for any cell close to plus or minus one between two different features.

11
00:01:03,014 --> 00:01:07,752
No pairwise correlation here is strong, a good first sign.

12
00:01:08,552 --> 00:01:10,391
Step two computes V I F.

13
00:01:10,391 --> 00:01:22,194
There is no single vectorised command, so we call variance inflation factor once per column with a short list comprehension, the standard idiom in Python.

14
00:01:22,194 --> 00:01:33,307
The V I Fs are one point three six for sales, one point oh four for quantity, one point oh six for discount and one point three seven for profit.

15
00:01:33,307 --> 00:01:41,048
All are close to one, well under five, so it is safe to use all four features together in clustering.

16
00:01:41,848 --> 00:01:44,764
Before clustering, scale the features.

17
00:01:44,764 --> 00:01:55,813
Standard Scaler rescales every feature to mean zero and standard deviation one, and fit transform learns the scaling and applies it in one step.

18
00:01:55,813 --> 00:02:08,704
Without scaling, sales, up to twenty two thousand dollars, would completely dominate discount, which runs zero to point eight, and K-Means would cluster on sales alone.

19
00:02:09,504 --> 00:02:12,785
Now implement K-Means with three clusters.

20
00:02:12,785 --> 00:02:16,926
Random state forty two makes the result reproducible.

21
00:02:16,926 --> 00:02:21,300
N init of ten runs ten random starts and keeps the best.

22
00:02:21,300 --> 00:02:26,769
Fit predict fits the model and returns each order's group in one step.

23
00:02:26,769 --> 00:02:40,440
The result is three very different group sizes, about sixty nine hundred, two thousand and one thousand seventy orders, already hinting at genuinely different kinds of orders.

24
00:02:41,240 --> 00:02:46,686
To choose k, loop over k from two to six and print the silhouette score.

25
00:02:46,686 --> 00:02:52,055
This is the one place a loop is the simplest way to compare candidates.

26
00:02:52,055 --> 00:03:01,963
The scores are zero point five oh four, point four seven three, point four three one, point four three seven and point three eight.

27
00:03:01,963 --> 00:03:11,720
k equals two scores highest, but k equals three is a close second and gives three interpretable groups, so we proceed with three.

28
00:03:12,520 --> 00:03:25,048
Colour a scatterplot of sales against profit by cluster using the hue argument, and draw a pairplot with hue to see every pair of features coloured by cluster, in one command.

29
00:03:25,848 --> 00:03:28,871
Name the groups with grouped statistics:

30
00:03:28,871 --> 00:03:32,573
group by cluster and average all numeric columns.

31
00:03:32,573 --> 00:03:41,641
Cluster zero is everyday orders, cluster one is heavy-discount, loss-making, and cluster two is high-value, high-profit.

32
00:03:41,641 --> 00:03:50,784
Then cross-tabulate clusters against category with row percentages to sanity check that the groups make real-world sense.

33
00:03:51,584 --> 00:03:54,323
The same pattern works on Titanic.

34
00:03:54,323 --> 00:04:00,607
All four features, age, fare and family counts, come back with V I F near one.

35
00:04:00,607 --> 00:04:08,180
The silhouette score for three clusters is about zero point four eight, similar to Superstore.

36
00:04:08,180 --> 00:04:22,520
One cluster is older, high-fare passengers with few family members; another is younger passengers travelling with larger families; the third is mid-age, low-fare solo travellers.

37
00:04:23,320 --> 00:04:24,842
Your cheat sheet.

38
00:04:24,842 --> 00:04:26,453
Multicollinearity:

39
00:04:26,453 --> 00:04:31,645
correlation heatmap, then V I F with a list comprehension.

40
00:04:31,645 --> 00:04:32,362
K-Means:

41
00:04:32,362 --> 00:04:39,881
scale with Standard Scaler, fit K Means, assign clusters, and score with silhouette.

42
00:04:39,881 --> 00:04:42,030
Visualise and interpret:

43
00:04:42,030 --> 00:04:48,296
scatterplot and pairplot with hue, and group by cluster for the means.

44
00:04:48,296 --> 00:04:50,714
Try it on your own dataset.

45
00:04:50,714 --> 00:04:52,504
Thanks for watching.

